# Boku no Natsuyasumi (SCPS-10088): 16-bit little-endian codes (72 00 = ぁ), keys in memory byte order.
# A code is the number of a glyph in the game's one font (1,488 cells of 12x12): 0x0000 a space, 0x0001-0x0033
# punctuation and signs, 0x0034-0x0071 digits and Latin letters, 0x0072-0x00C2 hiragana, 0x00C3-0x0116 katakana,
# 0x0117-0x05C8 kanji in the game's own order.
# Read by eye from the font, then proofread against the game's text: the dialogue and lists in BOKU.BIN and the
# menu texts in the executable are stored as these codes, uncompressed, and each kanji found in a sentence was
# read there. About 95 of the 1,202 kanji are in the font but in no text that was found; those are read from
# the glyph alone.
# The dialogue is set in columns, so the font draws ー 〜 … and the brackets turned. 0x000D is the long mark the
# dialogue uses; 0x000E is a level bar that the menus, set in rows, use for the same mark, so both are ー here.
# The menus write an ellipsis as two 0x0004. 0x05C2 and 0x05C3 are the word SELECT drawn across two cells.
# Not mapped: 0x05B0 (a mark not made out), 0x05C9 (a dashed line), 0x05CA+0x05CB and 0x05CD+0x05CE (a triangle
# and a circle drawn across two cells); no text found uses them.
# Commands: 0x8000 ends a text, 0x8001 starts a new line (the dialogue follows it with a space to indent), and
# 0x8002 ends a page; one more word follows 0x8002, a time (about three times the page's characters), not text.
